<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="monoterpene biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3041" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Arabidopsis thaliana col, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Terpenes comprise a very large class of plant secondary metabolites that serves a variety of different functions in basic and specialized metabolism. The majority of these compounds are likely to operate as direct defense agents because they often exhibit toxic and deterrent effects against plant pathogens and herbivores. However, since all monoterpenes, many sesquiterpenes and a few diterpenes have high vapor pressure at ambient temperature and volatize easily, these compounds could also act at a distance from the plant, serving as attractants or deterrents for pollinators and herbivores, or as signals released after damage by herbivores to attract their predators and parasitoids. In fact, volatile monterpenes and sesquiterpenes have frequently been shown to be emitted from flowers and other aerial parts of the plant, and their release is often developmentally regulated or induced by damage |CITS: [12724546],[15310830],[14756770]|. Curiously,volatile monoterpenes and sesquiterpenes have also been reported to be synthesized and accumulated in roots and rhizomes of various plant species |CITS: [12214699]|. As a consequence of their antimicrobial and antiherbivore activity, these substances may serve as direct defenses of below-ground tissue when they accumulate. &lt;b&gt;Structural Information&lt;/b&gt; Monoterpenes contain a 10-carbon backbone and are all synthesized from the common precursor geranyl diphosphate by the action of various monoterpene synthases. One characteristic feature of most terpene synthases (TPSs; monoterpenes, diterpenes, sesquiterpenes) is the formation of multiple products from a single substrate |CITS: [9442047]|. Comparative sequence analyses have shown that, within the same plant genome, TPSs making different products may be more similar to each other than TPSs making the same product in different species. &lt;b&gt;Commercial Uses&lt;/b&gt; There is an increasing need to find sustainable and renewable liquid fuels in the face of concerns over energy security and global petroleum supply. Monoterpenes are currently being exploited to determine their feasibilty as alternative fuels. Several isoprenoid based biofuels have shown great potential so far. Of these, pinene and limonene have recently been highlighted |CITS: [22895337]|. Pinene can be dimerized to yield a high density fuel and limonene is a natural potential biofuel. 1,8-cineole can be used in an 8:1 blend with gasoline.</Description>
