    <DataNode elementId="AT4G16740-MONOMER" textLabel="AT4G16740-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="A4FVP2" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The TPS03 terpene synthase from the Col ecotype of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; is a multifunctional enzyme capable of acting on both |FRAME: GERANYL-PP| and |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| during &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays. However, it appears that the enzyme has more activity as an &lt;i&gt;(E,E)&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;alpha;-farnesene synthase &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;. This may be due to substrate availability based on the subcellular localization of the enzyme. TPS03 does not have any putative N-terminal plastid transit peptide that would direct it to the choloroplast, nor does it have any other obvious motifs that would cause it to be targeted to another organelle. In fact, a cytoplasmic localization was suggested by a GFP fusion protein containing the first 50 amino acids of TPS03. The pools of the |FRAME: CPD-8764| precursor |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| derived from the |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY| are much higher in the chloroplast than the pools of FPP |CITS: [20463089]|. Transcripts for the TPS03 gene are upregulated in Col leaves following treatment with the jasmonic acid mimic coronalon or in response to the insect &lt;i&gt;Plutella xylostella&lt;/i&gt;. TPS03 transcripts can also be detected in flowers |CITS: [20463089]|. Maximum enzyme activity was achieved in the presence of |FRAME: MG+2|, whereas 40 mM |FRAME: K+| did not influence enzymatic activity |CITS: [20463089]|. Interestingly, two different ecotypes of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; show genetic and enzymatic changes at this locus. The Ws ecotype encodes a non-functional protein at the corresponding locus and produces a different range of volatile emissions |CITS: [20463089]|. On the other hand, a very similar protein is encoded in the genome of the C24 ecotype of &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. It differs only at one amino acid, with a phenylalanine replacing serine 267 in the Col protein sequence. However, its substrate specificity seems quite different |CITS: [20463089]|. &lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;beta;-ocimene is the main product generated by the C24 TPS03 enzyme (94%). Trace amounts of the related acyclic monoterpenes (&lt;i&gt;Z&lt;/i&gt;)-&amp;beta;-ocimene (4%) and myrcene (2%) were also found to be synthesized |CITS: [12624761]|. However, in marked contrast to the Col TPS03 enzyme, the C24 TPS03 does not appear to have &lt;i&gt;(E,E)&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;alpha;-farnesene synthase activity |CITS: [12624761][20463089]|. The pI of the Ws version of the protein was determined to be 6.65 |CITS: [12624761]|. A partial sequence for the C24 ecotype sequence can be obtained at &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q9FVI5&quot;&gt;UniProt&lt;/A&gt;.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G16740-MONOMER" />
